{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:29:02.360Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"31","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"FIXED. Reproduced by parsing the exact source from the finding and printing the emitted qualified names and IDs (throwaway probe over TreeSitterParser{}.ParseWithStatus + entitySymbols), not by reading the code.\n\n`class A { m(x){ const cb = () =\u003e { function helper(v){ return v } } } }` alone, before the fix:\n  function cb         -\u003e local/test:JavaScript:w.js:function:cb\n  function cb.helper  -\u003e local/test:JavaScript:w.js:function:cb.helper\nadding an identical class B:\n  A's helper -\u003e local/test:JavaScript:w.js:function:cb.helper#sig:80cfac553042146c\n  B's helper -\u003e local/test:JavaScript:w.js:function:cb.helper#sig:80cfac553042146c#2\nSo the finding is exact: both classes emitted `cb.helper` and adding the second moved the first onto a `#sig:` suffix.\n\nCAUSE: `childScope = entity.Name` took that name even when it was not qualified by the scope. `const cb = () =\u003e {}` is a variable_declarator, which never consults the scope, so its entity.Name is the bare `cb` — the walk dropped the `A.m` it had already established and re-anchored the callback body to a name that says nothing about which class it is in.\n\nNOT issue #214. #214 is the overload-set disambiguator, where the only two discriminators are signature and source position and each dies on one class of ordinary edit, so no syntactic key exists. Here the two `helper`s sit in structurally distinct lexical containers and the containment path names them uniquely — determined, not underdetermined. So this is fixed rather than dismissed.\n\nFIX (internal/sem/parser.go, walkEntitiesScoped): only a callable whose own name extends the scope may replace it —\n    if !scopeIsCallable || strings.HasPrefix(entity.Name, scope+\".\") { childScope = entity.Name }\nWhere it does not extend, the scope already holds the nearest enclosing callable's qualified name and is kept. After: A's helper = local/test:JavaScript:w.js:function:A.m.helper (bare, container local/test:JavaScript:w.js:method:A.m), B's = ...:function:B.m.helper (bare) — identical whether or not class B exists.\n\nPROOF: new regression test TestNestedCallableInsideAnUnqualifiedCallableKeepsTheEnclosingScope (JavaScript + TypeScript) in internal/sem/function_binding_test.go. Compile-clean revert = restore the unconditional `childScope = entity.Name` (nothing else; `go build ./...` clean, \"REVERT COMPILES CLEAN\"), then `go test ./internal/sem/ -run TestNestedCallableInsideAnUnqualifiedCallableKeepsTheEnclosingScope -v`:\n\n  --- FAIL: TestNestedCallableInsideAnUnqualifiedCallableKeepsTheEnclosingScope/JavaScript\n      function_binding_test.go:684: adding the same callback to B.m moved A.m's helper: local/r:JavaScript:w.js:function:cb.helper -\u003e local/r:JavaScript:w.js:function:cb.helper#sig:80cfac553042146c\n      function_binding_test.go:687: helper ID = local/r:JavaScript:w.js:function:cb.helper, want the callable-qualified local/r:JavaScript:w.js:function:A.m.helper\n      function_binding_test.go:692: nested container = \"local/r:JavaScript:w.js:function:cb\", want the enclosing method \"local/r:JavaScript:w.js:method:A.m\"\n  --- FAIL: .../TypeScript\n      function_binding_test.go:684: ... local/r:TypeScript:w.ts:function:cb.helper -\u003e local/r:TypeScript:w.ts:function:cb.helper#sig:20f89fe7ff96fd1d\n\nBOUNDARIES re-measured, all unchanged: `cb`'s own published name stays the bare `cb`; a top-level `function outer(){ function inner(){} }` stays local/test:JavaScript:w.js:function:inner; a TypeScript `namespace` establishes no scope at all, so the guard `scope != \"\"` never fires there; a class static block's `const cb` (scope is a TYPE, not a callable) still hands off to `cb` as before.\n\nOBSERVED WHILE MEASURING, NOT FIXED: `cb` itself carries the identical instability — local/test:JavaScript:w.js:function: